---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert creator strategist, audience-development consultant and content-system planner.

TASK:
Build a clear, sustainable set of content pillars for the supplied creator, personal brand, business expert or niche account.

The goal is to create a content structure that:
- serves real audience needs
- reflects the creator's expertise
- supports business or creator goals
- prevents random posting
- creates repeatable topic categories
- works across multiple content formats
- can be sustained over time

Do NOT create vague pillars such as:
Motivation
Tips
Lifestyle
Value

unless they are made specific to the creator's niche and audience.

CREATOR / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

NICHE:
[Main topic or industry.]

EXPERTISE:
[What can the creator genuinely teach, explain, demonstrate or discuss?]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who is the content for?]

AUDIENCE KNOWLEDGE LEVEL:
[Beginner / intermediate / advanced / mixed.]

AUDIENCE PROBLEMS:
[List known problems.]

AUDIENCE QUESTIONS:
[List common questions if known.]

AUDIENCE DESIRED OUTCOMES:
[What do they want to achieve?]

CREATOR GOALS:
[Authority / audience growth / leads / sales / community / education / monetization / portfolio / other.]

BUSINESS / MONETIZATION MODEL:
[Services / products / affiliate / sponsorship / ads / digital products / membership / none / other.]

PRIMARY PLATFORMS:
[Instagram / Facebook / TikTok / YouTube / LinkedIn / blog / email / other.]

PREFERRED CONTENT FORMATS:
[Short video / long-form video / carousel / article / email / live / podcast / other.]

POSTING CAPACITY:
[For example: 3 posts per week.]

CONTENT CREATION RESOURCES:
[Phone / camera / editing software / AI tools / team / solo / limited time / other.]

CREATOR STYLE:
[Professional / educational / entertaining / practical / conversational / premium / personal / other.]

TOPICS THE CREATOR WANTS TO COVER:
[List.]

TOPICS TO AVOID:
[List.]

EXISTING CONTENT:
[Optional: describe past topics or high-performing content.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

CONTENT PILLAR REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH THE AUDIENCE

2. IDENTIFY THE AUDIENCE'S MOST IMPORTANT:
Problems
Questions
Goals
Objections
Decisions
Tasks

3. DO NOT BUILD PILLARS ONLY AROUND WHAT THE CREATOR WANTS TO TALK ABOUT

4. CONNECT CREATOR EXPERTISE TO AUDIENCE NEEDS

5. IDENTIFY THE CREATOR'S CREDIBLE KNOWLEDGE AREAS

6. DO NOT POSITION THE CREATOR AS AN EXPERT IN AREAS NOT SUPPORTED BY THE PROVIDED INFORMATION

7. IDENTIFY THE MAIN CONTENT TERRITORIES

8. GROUP RELATED TOPICS INTO CLEAR PILLARS

9. A GOOD PILLAR SHOULD BE:
Specific
Broad enough for repeated content
Relevant to the audience
Connected to creator expertise
Strategically useful

10. AVOID PILLARS THAT ARE TOO BROAD

11. EXAMPLE OF TOO BROAD:
"Business"

12. BETTER:
"Small-Business Inventory Control"

13. AVOID PILLARS THAT ARE TOO NARROW

14. EXAMPLE OF TOO NARROW:
"How to fix one specific Excel formula"

15. THAT IS BETTER USED AS A TOPIC UNDER A BROADER PILLAR

16. RECOMMEND A MANAGEABLE NUMBER OF PILLARS

17. DEFAULT TO 3–6 CORE PILLARS UNLESS THE CONTEXT SUPPORTS MORE

18. DO NOT CREATE TEN PILLARS JUST TO LOOK COMPREHENSIVE

19. DEFINE THE PURPOSE OF EACH PILLAR

20. POSSIBLE PURPOSES:
Education
Discovery
Authority
Engagement
Trust
Conversion
Retention
Community

21. EACH PILLAR MAY SUPPORT MORE THAN ONE PURPOSE

22. IDENTIFY THE AUDIENCE NEED EACH PILLAR SERVES

23. IDENTIFY THE CREATOR GOAL EACH PILLAR SUPPORTS

24. CREATE SUBTOPICS UNDER EACH PILLAR

25. SUBTOPICS SHOULD BE REUSABLE

26. POSSIBLE SUBTOPIC TYPES:
How-to
Mistakes
FAQs
Processes
Tools
Frameworks
Examples
Comparisons
Stories
Case examples
Reviews
Behind the scenes
Opinions
Trends when verified

27. DO NOT RELY ON CURRENT TRENDS UNLESS CURRENT INFORMATION IS PROVIDED

28. CREATE RECURRING CONTENT SERIES

29. SERIES SHOULD FIT NATURALLY UNDER A PILLAR

30. EXAMPLES:
"Warehouse Mistake of the Week"
"AI Tool in 60 Seconds"
"Before You Buy"
"Quick Inventory Fix"

31. DO NOT FORCE A SERIES NAME IF IT SOUNDS artificial.

32. CREATE CONTENT ANGLES FOR EACH PILLAR

33. POSSIBLE ANGLES:
Beginner explanation
Common mistake
Question
Demonstration
Checklist
Comparison
Opinion
Story
Case example
Problem solving
Myth correction

34. CREATE A MIX OF AWARENESS STAGES

35. DISCOVERY CONTENT:
Broad but relevant problems
Questions
Mistakes
Quick insights

36. DEPTH CONTENT:
Processes
Frameworks
Detailed explanations
Case examples

37. TRUST CONTENT:
Experience
Methodology
Behind the scenes
Transparent lessons

38. CONVERSION-SUPPORT CONTENT:
Use cases
Product demonstrations
Service explanations
FAQs
Objection handling

39. DO NOT MAKE EVERY PILLAR SALES-ORIENTED

40. CREATE CONTENT THAT CAN ATTRACT NEW PEOPLE

41. CREATE CONTENT THAT HELPS EXISTING FOLLOWERS

42. CREATE CONTENT THAT BUILDS CREDIBILITY

43. CREATE CONTENT THAT SUPPORTS BUSINESS GOALS WHEN RELEVANT

44. IDENTIFY THE CREATOR'S DIFFERENTIATION

45. ASK:
What perspective can this creator credibly offer that is more specific than generic internet advice?

46. DIFFERENTIATION MAY COME FROM:
Professional experience
Industry specialization
Geographic knowledge
Process expertise
Creative style
Practical examples
Audience specialization

47. DO NOT INVENT DIFFERENTIATORS

48. IDENTIFY CONTENT OVERLAP

49. REMOVE PILLARS THAT COVER NEARLY THE SAME material.

50. EXPLAIN THE DISTINCTION BETWEEN SIMILAR PILLARS

51. CREATE A PILLAR BOUNDARY

52. FOR EACH PILLAR DEFINE:
What belongs here
What does not belong here

53. THIS HELPS PREVENT TOPIC CONFUSION

54. CREATE FORMAT MATCHING

55. IDENTIFY WHICH FORMATS WORK NATURALLY FOR EACH pillar.

56. EXAMPLE:
Process pillar → tutorials, screen recordings, demonstrations
Opinion pillar → talking-head videos, LinkedIn posts
Reference pillar → carousels, checklists, downloadable guides

57. DO NOT FORCE EVERY PILLAR INTO EVERY FORMAT

58. CREATE PLATFORM MATCHING

59. IDENTIFY WHICH PILLARS ARE STRONGEST FOR EACH platform.

60. DO NOT ASSUME ALL PLATFORMS NEED IDENTICAL CONTENT

61. CREATE SHORT-FORM POSSIBILITIES

62. CREATE LONG-FORM POSSIBILITIES

63. CREATE EMAIL OR NEWSLETTER POSSIBILITIES WHEN RELEVANT

64. CREATE SEARCH-ORIENTED TOPICS WHEN BLOG OR YOUTUBE IS RELEVANT

65. DO NOT INVENT SEARCH VOLUME

66. CREATE CONTENT DEPTH LEVELS

67. BEGINNER:
Definitions
Basic mistakes
Simple processes
FAQs

68. INTERMEDIATE:
Workflows
Comparisons
Troubleshooting
Decision-making

69. ADVANCED:
Trade-offs
Optimization
Strategy
Complex cases

70. BALANCE LEVELS BASED ON AUDIENCE

71. CREATE A CONTENT MIX

72. EXAMPLE:
40% education
25% discovery
15% authority
10% engagement
10% conversion-support

73. DO NOT PRESENT A UNIVERSAL IDEAL RATIO

74. ADAPT THE MIX TO THE CREATOR'S GOALS

75. CREATE A POSTING MODEL

76. MATCH IT TO REALISTIC CAPACITY

77. EXAMPLE FOR 3 POSTS PER WEEK:
Post 1 — Education pillar
Post 2 — Discovery / problem pillar
Post 3 — Authority / trust / conversion-support rotation

78. DO NOT RECOMMEND DAILY POSTING IF THE CREATOR CANNOT SUSTAIN IT

79. CREATE A ROTATION SYSTEM

80. ROTATION SHOULD PREVENT:
Overposting one topic
Neglecting important pillars
Constant selling

81. CREATE CONTENT BATCHING OPPORTUNITIES

82. GROUP CONTENT THAT CAN BE CREATED TOGETHER

83. EXAMPLE:
Record 4 videos from one pillar in one session.

84. CREATE REPURPOSING OPPORTUNITIES

85. SHOW HOW ONE DEEP TOPIC CAN PRODUCE:
Short video
Carousel
Post
Email
Article
FAQ

86. DO NOT CREATE DUPLICATE CONTENT WITHOUT ADAPTATION

87. IDENTIFY EVERGREEN PILLARS

88. IDENTIFY TIMELY / CURRENT PILLARS WHEN APPLICABLE

89. CURRENT PILLARS SHOULD REQUIRE FACT CHECKING

90. CREATE A CONTENT IDEA BANK FOR EACH PILLAR

91. PROVIDE MULTIPLE EXAMPLES

92. DO NOT CREATE GENERIC TITLES

93. MAKE TITLES SPECIFIC TO THE AUDIENCE AND NICHE

94. CREATE QUESTION-BASED IDEAS

95. CREATE PROBLEM-BASED IDEAS

96. CREATE MISTAKE-BASED IDEAS

97. CREATE HOW-TO IDEAS

98. CREATE CASE / EXAMPLE IDEAS

99. CREATE OPINION OR PERSPECTIVE IDEAS WHEN CREDIBLE

100. CREATE CONTENT FOR DIFFERENT FUNNEL STAGES

101. CREATE NON-PROMOTIONAL VALUE CONTENT

102. IDENTIFY WHICH PILLARS COULD NATURALLY SUPPORT:
Service
Product
Affiliate
Download
Newsletter
Consultation

103. DO NOT FORCE MONETIZATION INTO EVERY TOPIC

104. CREATE CTA MATCHING

105. DISCOVERY CTA:
Follow
Save
Share

106. ENGAGEMENT CTA:
Comment
Answer
Share experience

107. DEPTH CTA:
Read
Watch
Download

108. CONVERSION CTA:
Try
Book
Buy
Enquire

109. USE ONLY CTAS THAT ACTUALLY EXIST

110. CREATE AN AUTHORITY STRATEGY

111. IDENTIFY WHICH PILLARS BEST SHOW:
Knowledge
Experience
Process
Judgment
Results when verified

112. DO NOT CREATE UNSUPPORTED AUTHORITY CLAIMS

113. CREATE A TRUST STRATEGY

114. TRUST MAY COME FROM:
Transparent explanations
Real examples
Behind-the-scenes process
Limitations
Mistakes learned
Verified results

115. DO NOT INVENT CASE STUDIES OR RESULTS

116. CREATE A PERSONAL CONTENT ROLE IF APPROPRIATE

117. PERSONAL CONTENT SHOULD SUPPORT:
Connection
Context
Perspective
Story

118. DO NOT FORCE PERSONAL-LIFE CONTENT INTO A PROFESSIONAL BRAND

119. CREATE COMMUNITY CONTENT

120. COMMUNITY CONTENT MAY INCLUDE:
Questions
Polls
Audience experiences
Challenges
Feedback
Responses

121. DO NOT USE EMPTY ENGAGEMENT BAIT

122. CREATE CONTENT-GAP ANALYSIS

123. IDENTIFY:
Audience needs not covered
Overrepresented topics
Missing funnel stages
Missing depth
Missing formats

124. CREATE PILLAR PRIORITY

125. RANK PILLARS BY:
Audience value
Creator credibility
Strategic importance
Content depth
Sustainability
Monetization fit when relevant

126. USE:
Core
Supporting
Experimental

127. DO NOT CREATE FALSE NUMERICAL SCORES

128. CREATE EXPERIMENTAL PILLARS CAREFULLY

129. EXPERIMENTAL PILLARS MAY TEST:
New topic
New format
Adjacent audience need

130. DO NOT ALLOW EXPERIMENTAL TOPICS TO DILUTE THE BRAND WITHOUT reason.

131. CREATE A 30-DAY CONTENT MAP

132. DISTRIBUTE IDEAS ACROSS THE CORE PILLARS

133. MATCH PLAN TO POSTING CAPACITY

134. DO NOT FORCE 30 POSTS IF THE CREATOR POSTS THREE times per week.

135. CREATE A 90-DAY DIRECTION

136. FOCUS ON:
Consistency
Learning
Audience response
Topic refinement

137. DO NOT LOCK THE CREATOR INTO A RIGID PLAN

138. CREATE MEASUREMENT GUIDANCE

139. TRACK BY PILLAR:
Reach
Saves
Shares
Comments
Qualified clicks
Leads
Conversions
Watch time
Retention

140. MATCH METRICS TO THE PILLAR PURPOSE

141. DO NOT JUDGE EVERY PILLAR ONLY BY VIEWS

142. AUTHORITY CONTENT MAY HAVE DIFFERENT SUCCESS SIGNALS THAN DISCOVERY CONTENT

143. CREATE A PILLAR REVIEW PROCESS

144. AFTER ENOUGH CONTENT IS PUBLISHED:
Review performance
Review audience feedback
Identify strongest topics
Adjust pillar weight

145. DO NOT REMOVE A PILLAR AFTER ONE WEAK POST

146. CREATE A CONTENT DECISION FILTER

147. BEFORE POSTING ASK:
Which pillar does this belong to?
Which audience problem does it address?
What is the objective?
Why should the audience care?
What format best communicates it?

148. IF THE IDEA DOES NOT FIT ANY PILLAR
Decide whether it is:
A useful experiment
Off-brand
A sign that a new pillar is needed

149. CREATE NAMING OPTIONS

150. PILLAR NAMES SHOULD BE CLEAR TO THE CREATOR

151. THEY DO NOT NEED TO BE PUBLIC-FACING BRAND SLOGANS

152. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- pillars are audience-centered
- pillars reflect real creator expertise
- pillars are distinct from one another
- pillars are broad enough for repeated content
- pillars are specific enough to guide topic selection
- each pillar has a strategic purpose
- content formats match the pillar
- platform differences are considered
- content can be produced with available resources
- posting frequency is sustainable
- discovery, depth, trust and conversion are balanced
- monetization is not forced
- no expertise is invented
- no results are invented
- no current trends are invented
- content gaps are identified
- strong pillars are prioritized
- the system can evolve based on performance

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. CREATOR STRATEGY SNAPSHOT
Provide:
Niche
Audience
Audience problems
Audience desired outcomes
Creator strengths
Creator goals
Recommended strategic direction

2. RECOMMENDED CONTENT PILLARS

For each pillar provide:
Pillar name
Purpose
Audience need
Creator strength
What belongs in this pillar
What does not belong
Best formats
Best platforms
Funnel role
Priority

3. SUBTOPICS
Provide 5–10 reusable subtopics for each pillar.

4. CONTENT IDEA BANK
Generate practical content ideas under every pillar.

For each idea provide:
Title
Angle
Format
Objective
Audience stage

5. RECURRING SERIES
Recommend repeatable series under the strongest pillars.

6. CONTENT MIX
Recommend a practical percentage or relative weighting based on the creator's goals.

Explain that it is a starting point rather than a universal rule.

7. POSTING ROTATION
Create a sustainable rotation based on the stated posting capacity.

8. PLATFORM MAP
Show which pillars are strongest for each platform.

9. FORMAT MAP
Show where to use:
Short video
Long-form video
Carousel
Post
Email
Article
Live content

10. CONTENT BATCHING PLAN
Show which content can be produced together efficiently.

11. REPURPOSING MAP
Show how one strong topic can become multiple platform-native assets.

12. MONETIZATION ALIGNMENT
Where relevant, explain which pillars naturally support:
Products
Services
Affiliate content
Downloads
Sponsorship
Lead generation

Do not force monetization where it does not fit.

13. AUTHORITY & TRUST PLAN
Identify which pillars best demonstrate expertise and trust.

14. CONTENT GAP ANALYSIS
Identify missing:
Problems
Questions
Formats
Funnel stages
Depth levels

15. 30-DAY CONTENT MAP
Create a realistic plan based on posting capacity.

16. 90-DAY DIRECTION
Explain how to develop the strongest pillars over time.

17. MEASUREMENT PLAN
For each pillar provide:
Purpose
Primary metric
Secondary metric
What to learn

18. PILLAR REVIEW FRAMEWORK
Explain how to decide whether to:
Increase
Maintain
Reduce
Test
Replace

a pillar over time.

19. CONTENT DECISION FILTER
Provide a short checklist the creator can use before creating any new piece of content.

20. FINAL RECOMMENDATION
Provide:
3 strongest pillars
Best discovery pillar
Best authority pillar
Best trust pillar
Best monetization-support pillar if relevant
Best first content series

IMPORTANT:
- Build pillars around audience needs and real creator expertise.
- Do not create vague categories that fail to guide content decisions.
- Do not invent expertise, case studies, results or trends.
- Do not force every post toward conversion.
- Make the system sustainable for the creator's actual production capacity.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Connect expertise to audience need",
    description:
      "The strongest pillars sit where the creator's genuine knowledge overlaps with problems, questions and outcomes that matter to the audience.",
  },
  {
    title: "Keep the pillars distinct",
    description:
      "Each pillar should have a clear boundary so the creator knows which topics belong there and avoids building several categories around essentially the same idea.",
  },
  {
    title: "Give every pillar a job",
    description:
      "Some pillars can attract new people, others can build depth, authority, trust or support conversion. A healthy content system does not make every post perform the same role.",
  },
  {
    title: "Design for sustainability",
    description:
      "Match the content mix, formats and posting rotation to the creator's realistic production capacity rather than creating a plan that cannot be maintained.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Niche: Warehouse, inventory and operations management.",
    "Audience: Storekeepers, inventory controllers, supervisors and small-business operators.",
    "Expertise: Inventory control, warehouse processes, stock discrepancies, KPIs and practical operations.",
    "Goal: Build professional authority and drive traffic to practical online tools.",
    "Platforms: LinkedIn, Facebook and YouTube Shorts.",
    "Capacity: Three posts per week.",
  ],

  output: [
    "PILLAR 1 — Inventory Accuracy & Control: stock discrepancies, cycle counts, system-versus-physical differences, stock adjustments and root-cause checks.",
    "PILLAR 2 — Warehouse Process Improvement: receiving, putaway, picking, dispatch, layout and workflow problems.",
    "PILLAR 3 — Warehouse KPIs & Decision-Making: inventory accuracy, productivity, stock turnover, reorder planning and operational interpretation.",
    "PILLAR 4 — Practical Tools & Templates: calculators, checklists, reports and templates that help operators complete real tasks.",
    "PILLAR 5 — Career & Professional Practice: supervisor lessons, workplace problem-solving and practical skills for warehouse professionals.",
    "ROTATION: Week 1 could use one inventory-control educational post, one warehouse-process short video and one practical-tool demonstration rather than publishing three similar inventory posts.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Pillars are decision filters",
    description:
      "A useful pillar system makes it easier to decide what to publish, what to postpone and what does not belong on the account.",
  },
  {
    title: "Do not confuse pillars with formats",
    description:
      "A Reel, carousel or article is a format. The pillar is the recurring subject area that those formats communicate.",
  },
  {
    title: "Allow the system to evolve",
    description:
      "Pillars should provide consistency without becoming permanent rules. Audience response and creator goals can justify changing their weight over time.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Creator Content Pillar Builder Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Build a sustainable creator content strategy with clear content pillars, subtopics, recurring series, posting rotation and platform-specific ideas."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="CREATORS"
    title="Creator Content Pillar Builder"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Build clear, audience-focused content pillars that connect creator expertise with repeatable topics, platform formats, sustainable publishing and long-term goals."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Content strategy",
      "Creator planning",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Personal brands",
      "Content creators",
      "Professional creators",
      "Small businesses",
      "Social media planning",
      "Multi-platform content",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Replace random posting with a repeatable content system."
    introDescription="Define a small number of clear content territories around your audience, expertise and goals, then turn those pillars into reusable topics, series, formats and a realistic publishing rotation."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn warehouse expertise into a structured creator content system."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Good content pillars create direction without limiting creativity."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need ideas inside each pillar?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Short-Form Video Idea Generator and Hook Generator to turn each content pillar into specific concepts and platform-ready openings."
    warningTitle="Do not build strategy around invented expertise"
    warningText="Keep each content pillar connected to knowledge, experience or resources the creator can genuinely support. Verify current trends, performance claims and external facts separately when they are used in published content."
  />
</BaseLayout>